  "resources": ["eni-0123456789abc0002", "subnet-0123456789abc0001", "vpc-0123456789abc0001", "tgw-attach-0123456789abc0001", "10.0.1.5", "10.0.2.9", "account 012345678901_1"],
  "timeframe": "not time-critical; this is a config lookup, not a log time-range query",
  "criteria": "1) Identify the TGW (describe-transit-gateway-attachments / describe-transit-gateways) that owns tgw-attach-0123456789abc0001 and determine if it spans other accounts/VPCs. 2) Search other accessible accounts/VPCs for an ENI or instance with private IP 10.0.2.9 (describe-network-interfaces, describe-instances with the IP filter). 3) Once the true source instance/ENI (behind the TGW, originating 10.0.1.5 traffic) and the destination ENI (10.0.2.9) are identified, pull their security groups (describe-security-groups) and check: does the destination SG allow inbound TCP 3389 from 10.0.1.5/relevant source, and does the source SG allow outbound TCP 3389 to 10.0.2.9? 4) Pull the network ACLs for both subnets (describe-network-acls) and check inbound/outbound TCP 3389 rules in rule-number order for both CIDRs. 5) Also check TGW route tables (search-transit-gateway-routes) to confirm the path exists and isn't blocked at routing. 6) Report back: which specific rule (SG rule or NACL rule, with its exact rule number/ID and content) is responsible for the REJECT, or state clearly if it cannot be determined from available data, and list the exact read-only AWS CLI commands used so they can be reproduced."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Investigate SG vs NACL cause for a lone flow-log REJECT across a Transit Gateway"}], "created_at": null}

